  "prompt_text": "The figure is a compact design map for metal-coordinated polymer materials, showing how different molecular choices lead to different material forms. At the top, it highlights three common coordinating ligands—catechol, imidazole, and phosphate—as the functional groups that can bind crosslinking species. It then summarizes several synthetic routes, including exchange, RAFT, and ROP, suggesting multiple polymerization strategies can be used to introduce these ligands.\n\nThe polymers themselves are depicted in two main architectures: ligands placed along polymer side chains or positioned at chain ends in telechelic polymers. These ligand-bearing polymers can then be crosslinked either by metal ions or by nanoparticles. The resulting networks are shown in two physical states: a hydrated gel and a melt-like network, indicating that similar coordination chemistry can generate soft solid or fluid-processable materials depending on formulation.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure contrasts these tunable design variables with more traditional material handles such as concentration and molecular weight. Overall, the image conveys a modular framework: ligand type, synthesis method, polymer architecture, and crosslinker identity together control the structure and macroscopic behavior of coordination-based polymer networks.",
